{% case_body "I built it." %}
  <p>AI has made bits of it easier. It still wasn't <em>good</em>.</p>
  <p>A web app. Lives on my phone like a native one — saved to the home screen, opens in a tap. New plan arrives, I upload the <strong>PDF or CSV</strong>, and the app reads it, splits it into days, and lays the week out properly.</p>
  <p>Then it gets out of the way.</p>
  <p>I've spent enough years inside gym apps to know what I don't want from one. Most of them feel like they were built by someone who's <em>never</em> actually used them. Too rigid. No drop sets without three taps and a menu. Supersets buried. You can't just <strong>do the workout</strong>.</p>
  <p>So I built every flexibility I've ever missed:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Drop sets, supersets, rest-pause</strong> — first-class, not workarounds</li>
    <li><strong>Edit anything mid-session</strong> — reps, weight, swap an exercise, add one in</li>
    <li><strong>Last-week glance</strong> — the number you're chasing, right there on the row</li>
    <li><strong>Big numbers, one tap</strong> — readable mid-session, no squinting required</li>
    <li><strong>Plate stacking</strong> — tap each plate as you load it, see the total weight stack up in real time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It's an app built by someone who actually uses it. <em>Imagine that.</em></p>
{% endcase_body %}

